# AI Transparency Statement: Digital Scam Guardian (Prototype 1)

This document says plainly which tools and AI assistance were used to build this project, what the AI did, what the author did, and how the work was checked.

- **Author:** RAHINIE GUHA RAY
- **Project:** Digital Scam Guardian, Prototype 1
- **Date:** October 2026

## 1. Tools used

| Tool | How it was used |
|---|---|
| **Claude** (Anthropic AI assistant, via the claude.ai chat) | Planning, code review and code writing, test data, documentation, diagrams, the slide deck and the competition description. Also used its web search to find published statistics. |
| **GitHub** | Hosting the repository and version history. |
| **GitHub Codespaces** | The cloud development environment where all code was run and tested. |

No other AI assistants or tools were used.

## 2. Does the product itself use AI?

**Not generative AI.** The running prototype makes no calls to Claude or any other AI service, and no message text is sent anywhere.

| Component | What it is |
|---|---|
| Rules | Hand-written word and pattern rules (urgency, money requests, secrecy, PIN/OTP requests, threats, fake authority, prizes). |
| Sender check | A comparison with a small, **made-up** contact history stored in a local SQLite file. |
| Link check | Local rules on link shapes (short links, look-alike domains, odd endings). No network lookups. |
| Machine-learning helper | A small classical text classifier (TF-IDF with logistic regression, scikit-learn), trained at start-up on the **48 development messages** in this repository. It is used only as a weak hint and counted only when it is more than 50% confident. |
| Risk engine | Fixed arithmetic that combines the layers (noisy-OR). |

